  {"type": "split", "h2": "Sizing a tankless for Ontario groundwater", "image": "Hot water running at a bathroom sink", "image_src": "/assets/img/brands/rinnai-hot-water-sink.webp", "image_fit": "cover", "image_pos": "50% 50%", "image_credit": "Image: Rinnai", "html": "<p>Tankless sizing is about temperature rise, and that's where Ontario is different from the brochure. Incoming water in a Southern Ontario winter can drop into the single digits Celsius, and well water up north runs colder still. To deliver a 49°C shower from 5°C water, the heater has to raise the temperature by about 44°C, and a tankless unit's flow rate falls as the required rise climbs. The GPM number on the box is a best-case figure at a small rise.</p><p>So we size by what you'll actually run at once in February: a shower uses roughly 2 to 2.5 GPM, a kitchen tap about 1.5, a washing machine about 2. Two showers and a dishwasher at the same time is a job for the RX199iN; one shower and a tap is fine on the RX160iN. We count your fixtures, check your winter inlet temperature, and pick the model that still delivers when it's -20°C outside, not just in July.</p>"},
